      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I have this logic problem to solve, and I need to figure out whether the statement \"The rabbit does not see the lion\" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context. Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, I'll list out all the given premises and the implications to understand the relationships between the animals. The context provided is a series of statements, some of which are implications. I'll go through each one and see how they connect the animals: bear, squirrel, rabbit, and lion.\n\n1. The bear sees the squirrel.\n2. The bear visits the rabbit.\n3. The lion is not round.\n4. The lion does not like the rabbit.\n5. The lion visits the bear.\n6. The rabbit is not big.\n7. The rabbit is kind.\n8. The rabbit likes the squirrel.\n9. The rabbit sees the bear.\n10. The rabbit does not visit the bear.\n11. The squirrel visits the lion.\n12. If something is big and it visits the lion, then the lion visits the rabbit.\n13. If something sees the lion, then it does not like the lion.\n14. If something likes the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the rabbit.\n15. If something sees the squirrel and it visits the squirrel, then the squirrel does not see the lion.\n16. If something visits the lion, then the lion is not round.\n17. If something sees the lion and it does not see the bear, then it sees the squirrel.\n18. If something visits the rabbit, then it sees the lion.\n19. If something sees the lion, then it is big.\n\nWow, that's a lot. Let me try to parse these implications one by one.\n\nStarting with the premises:\n\n- The bear sees the squirrel (1).\n- The bear visits the rabbit (2).\n- The lion is not round (3).\n- The lion does not like the rabbit (4).\n- The lion visits the bear (5).\n- The rabbit is not big (6).\n- The rabbit is kind (7).\n- The rabbit likes the squirrel (8).\n- The rabbit sees the bear (9).\n- The rabbit does not visit the bear (10).\n- The squirrel visits the lion (11).\n\nNow, the impli