If something needs the squirrel and the squirrel needs the cow then the cow does not need the rabbit. So, if X needs the squirrel and the squirrel needs the cow, then the cow doesn't need the rabbit. But again, this is about the cow needing the rabbit, not the rabbit needing the cow.\n\nStatement 18: If the cow needs the rabbit then the rabbit does not eat the mouse. So, if the cow needs the rabbit, then the rabbit doesn't eat the mouse. But this doesn't directly say anything about the rabbit needing the cow.\n\nStatement 17: If the mouse eats the rabbit and the rabbit does not see the mouse, then the rabbit is green. So, if the mouse eats the rabbit and the rabbit doesn't see the mouse, then the rabbit is green. But I don't know if the mouse eats the rabbit yet.\n\nStatement 19: If something eats the squirrel and the squirrel eats the cow then it needs the cow. So, if X eats the squirrel and the squirrel eats the cow, then X needs the cow. But I don't know if the rabbit does that.\n\nStatement 20: If something eats the mouse then it eats the squirrel. So, if X eats the mouse, then X eats the squirrel. But I don't know if the rabbit eats the mouse.\n\nWait, statement 6 says the rabbit eats the mouse. So the rabbit does eat the mouse. Now, from statement 20, if something eats the mouse, it eats the squirrel. So, since the rabbit eats the mouse, does that mean the rabbit eats the squirrel? That seems to be the case. So the rabbit eats the mouse and the squirrel.\n\nBut let's see what else we can find. Statement 14: If something sees the rabbit then it eats the cow. So, if X sees the rabbit, then X eats the cow. But the rabbit is not blue (statement 7), and the squirrel is not blue (statement 8), but the squirrel is green (statement 9). So the squirrel is green.\n\nStatement 12: The squirrel sees the rabbit. So the squirrel sees the rabbit. From statement 14, if something sees the rabbit, it eats the cow. So the squirrel sees the rabbit, which would mean the squirrel eats the cow. But wait, statement 3 says the mouse eats the cow. So the mouse eats the cow, and the squirrel also eats the cow? That seems conflicting because if both the mouse and the squirrel eat the cow, but the mouse needs the squirrel (statement 5) and the squirrel needs the mouse (statement 11). So they need each other.\n\nWait, but statement 3 says the mouse eats the cow, and statement 19 says if something eats the squirrel and the squirrel eats the cow, then it needs the cow. So if the squirrel eats the cow, then the something needs the cow. But the squirrel does eat the cow because the squirrel sees the rabbit (statement 12), which makes the squirrel eat the cow (from 14). So the squirrel eats the cow, which means the squirrel needs the cow (from 19). So the squirrel needs the cow.\n\nBut the mouse also needs the squirrel (statement 5) and the squirrel needs the mouse (statement 11). So they need each other.\n\nNow, going back to statement 16: If something needs the squirrel and the squirrel needs the cow, then the cow does not need the rabbit. So, if X needs the squirrel and the squirrel needs the cow, then the cow doesn't need the rabbit. Since the squirrel needs the cow, and the squirrel is needed by the mouse, which is needed by the rabbit (statement 6: rabbit eats the mouse). Wait, the rabbit eats the mouse, so the mouse is eaten by the rabbit. But the mouse needs the squirrel, and the squirrel needs the mouse. So the rabbit eats the mouse, which is needed by the squirrel, which is needed by the mouse, which is needed by the rabbit? That seems a bit circular.\n\nWait, let me try to map this out:\n\n- The rabbit eats the mouse (6).\n- The mouse needs the squirrel (5).\n- The squirrel needs the mouse (11).\n- The squirrel sees the rabbit (12), so the squirrel eats the cow (14).\n- The squirrel eats the cow (14), so the squirrel needs the cow (19).\n- The squirrel needs the cow (19), and the squirrel is needed by the mouse (5 and 11), so from statement 16: