e is cold and sees the lion, they need the tiger. So, (cold \u2227 sees lion) \u2192 needs tiger.\n\nLet me try to connect these.\n\nFirst, does the mouse see the lion? Yes, from statement 4: mouse sees lion.\n\nSo, if the mouse sees the lion, then according to statement 11, the mouse is cold. So, mouse is cold.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's any other information that might affect this.\n\nFrom statement 16: If someone eats the mouse and the mouse sees the lion, then they are green. So, (eats mouse \u2227 mouse sees lion) \u2192 green.\n\nBut the mouse is the one being eaten, so who is the subject here? It says someone eats the mouse, and the mouse sees the lion. So, if someone eats the mouse, and the mouse sees the lion, then that someone is green.\n\nBut the mouse is the one seeing the lion, so the mouse sees the lion, but the someone is the eater of the mouse. So, if the eater of the mouse sees that the mouse sees the lion, then the eater is green.\n\nBut I'm not sure if that affects the mouse's coldness.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the mouse is the one who is cold because it sees the lion.\n\nWait, statement 11 says if someone sees the lion, they are cold. The mouse sees the lion, so the mouse is cold.\n\nBut let me check if there's any other condition that might affect this.\n\nFrom statement 17: If someone needs the tiger, then the tiger sees the lion. So, needs tiger \u2192 tiger sees lion.\n\nFrom statement 18: If someone is cold and sees the lion, they need the tiger.\n\nSo, if the mouse is cold (from seeing the lion), then according to statement 18, the mouse needs the tiger.\n\nBut from statement 9: The tiger needs the mouse. So, tiger needs mouse.\n\nSo, the tiger needs the mouse, and the mouse needs the tiger? Wait, no, the mouse doesn't necessarily need the tiger, unless it's implied.\n\nWait, the mouse sees the lion, which makes it cold, so it needs the tiger (from 18). So, mouse needs tiger.\n\nBut the tiger needs the mouse (from 9). So, it's a mutual need.\n\nBut does that affect the mouse's coldness? The mouse is cold because it sees the lion, so it needs the tiger. But the tiger needs the mouse, so that's consistent.\n\nSo, putting it all together:\n\n- Mouse sees lion (statement 4).\n- Seeing lion \u2192 cold (statement 11).\n- Therefore, mouse is cold.\n\nBut wait, let me check if there's any other condition that might make the mouse not cold.\n\nLooking at statement 12: If someone needs the tiger and needs the mouse, they are cold.\n\nSo, (needs tiger \u2227 needs mouse) \u2192 cold.\n\nThe mouse needs the tiger (from 18), and the tiger needs the mouse (from 9). So, both conditions are met: mouse needs tiger and tiger needs mouse. Therefore, according to statement 12, the mouse is cold.\n\nSo, both statements 11 and 12 lead to the conclusion that the mouse is cold.\n\nIs there any information that might contradict this? Let's see.\n\nFrom statement 16: If someone eats the mouse and the mouse sees the lion, then they are green.\n\nBut the mouse is being eaten, so the eater is green. But the mouse itself is cold, so that doesn't directly affect the mouse's coldness.\n\nAlso, from statement 14 and 15: All round people are green, and all young green people are round. But I'm not sure if that's relevant here because it's about people, not the mouse.\n\nWait, but the context doesn't specify whether the mouse is a person or an animal. Wait, the context starts with \"The cow eats the lion,\" which are animals, so the mouse is an animal. So, statements about people (round, green, etc.) might not apply to the mouse.\n\nTherefore, the mouse is an animal, so the statements about people (round, green) don't affect it.\n\nSo, the mouse is cold because it sees the lion, and also because it needs the tiger and the tiger needs the mouse, making it cold.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"The mouse is not cold\" is false because the mouse is cold.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I didn't miss anything.\n\nIs there any possibil