tement must be false.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the cat doesn't see the squirrel, so the implication in statement 20 doesn't apply. But statement 15 says that because the mouse eats the cat, the cat must visit the squirrel. So, the cat must see the squirrel, regardless of the squirrel's properties.\n\nBut then, the squirrel is round, so it can't be rough, which contradicts statement 20 when the cat sees the squirrel. Therefore, the only way to avoid the contradiction is if the statement is false.\n\nWait, but if the statement is false, then the cat doesn't see the squirrel. So, the implication in statement 15 would be that if the mouse eats the cat, the cat visits the squirrel. But if the cat doesn't see the squirrel, then the implication is trivially true because the antecedent is false. So, the statement \"The cat sees the squirrel\" is false because it leads to a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"The cat sees the squirrel\" leads to a contradiction because it implies the squirrel is both round and rough, which is impossible. Therefore, the statement is false.\n\nB) False",
