pply to him.\n\nAnother statement: If Gary is furry and not smart, then Gary is white. Gary is smart, so that doesn't apply. But Gary is smart, so he must be green (from smart people are green). So Gary is green and smart, which makes sense.\n\nNow, let's see about Bob's color. Since Bob is smart, he must be green. So Bob is green. But the statement says if Bob is not green and not white, then he's furry. But since Bob is green, that condition doesn't apply. So Bob is green, which makes him furry, as per the first statement.\n\nWait, but the context also says Bob is white? No, the context says Bob is big, cold, furry, and smart. It doesn't explicitly say he's white. So if Bob is green, then he's not white. Because Fiona is white, but Bob is green.\n\nWait, but the statement says if Bob is not green and not white, then he's furry. But since Bob is green, that condition isn't met, so we don't know if he's white or not from that. However, from the fact that smart people are green, Bob must be green, so he's not white.\n\nWait, but the context says Bob is white? No, the context doesn't say that. The context says Fiona is white, but not Bob. So Bob is green because he's smart, so he's not white. Therefore, the statement \"Bob is white\" is false.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. The context says:\n\n- Bob is big, cold, furry, smart.\n- Fiona is cold, not green, white.\n- Gary is smart.\n- Harry is big, cold, smart.\n- All green people are furry.\n- If Gary is furry and not smart, then Gary is white. But Gary is smart, so that doesn't apply.\n- Smart people are green, so Bob is green.\n- If Bob is not green and not white, then he's furry. But Bob is green, so that doesn't apply.\n- Big people are cold, which applies to Bob and Harry.\n- If someone is smart and furry, then they are big. Bob is smart and furry, so he's big, which matches.\n- If someone is white and not cold, then they are not blue. Fiona is white and cold, so that's fine.\n- All cold people are not blue. So Bob, being cold, is not blue.\n\nSo, Bob is green because he's smart. Therefore, he's not white. So the statement \"Bob is white\" is false.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Bob is white\" is false because Bob is green due to being smart, and the context does not indicate he is white.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
