. If something is nice, then it is not green.\n9. Quiet, blue things are big.\n10. If something is blue, then it is young.\n11. If something is nice, then it is quiet.\n12. Young things are blue.\n13. If Anne is young, then Anne is quiet.\n14. If Dave is young, then Dave is green.\n15. All big, quiet things are nice.\n16. If Fiona is blue, then Fiona is not green.\n\nSo, focusing on Dave:\n\n- Dave is big (5) and nice (6).\n- From 11: Nice \u2192 quiet. So, Dave is quiet.\n- Therefore, \"Dave is not quiet\" is false.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's any possibility that Dave is not quiet. For example, could Dave be nice without being quiet? But fact 11 says if something is nice, it is quiet. So, no, that's a direct implication. Therefore, Dave must be quiet.\n\nSo, the statement \"Dave is not quiet\" is false.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Dave is not quiet\" is false because, based on the given information, Dave is nice, and according to fact 11, nice things are quiet. Therefore, Dave must be quiet.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
