ps through the logical implications. Let me think.\n\nWe know that Gary is quiet, but that doesn't necessarily mean that others are not quiet. Unless there's a rule that says only Gary is quiet, but that's not stated.\n\nWait, the context doesn't provide any information about other people's quietness except Gary. So, unless we can derive it from the given statements, we can't say anything about Dave's quietness.\n\nWait, but let me check again. The question is whether \"Dave is not quiet\" is true, false, or unknown.\n\nFrom the context, we don't have any information that directly states whether Dave is quiet or not. The only person mentioned as quiet is Gary. So, unless there's an indirect implication, we can't determine if Dave is quiet or not.\n\nWait, but maybe through the logical implications. Let me see.\n\nWe know that Gary is quiet, but that doesn't affect Dave. Unless there's a rule that says only Gary is quiet, but that's not stated. So, we can't assume that Dave is not quiet just because Gary is.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps through the context, we can find that Dave is not quiet because of some other attribute. Let me think.\n\nWait, the context says that if something is round, then it is big (statement 9). Dave is round, so he's big. Also, from statement 8: All rough, big things are smart. Since Dave is rough and big, he's smart, which we already knew.\n\nBut does being big or smart have anything to do with being quiet? Not directly, unless there's a rule that connects them, which there isn't.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps through the context, we can find that Dave is not quiet because of his other attributes. But I don't see a direct link.\n\nWait, maybe through the fact that Gary is quiet, and Gary is white, but Dave is also white. Does that mean anything? Not necessarily. The context doesn't say that only white people are quiet or that quiet people have certain attributes.\n\nSo, in conclusion, the context doesn't provide any information about Dave's quietn