een people are furry. So, if someone is big and green, they are furry.\n\nStatement 17: If someone is smart and round, then they are quiet. So, (smart \u2227 round) \u2192 quiet.\n\nStatement 18: Green people are smart. So, green \u2192 smart.\n\nStatement 19: All smart, furry people are big. So, (smart \u2227 furry) \u2192 big.\n\nNow, let's try to connect these statements to see what we can deduce about each person.\n\nStarting with Charlie:\n\n- Charlie is big (statement 2).\n- From statement 12: big \u2192 smart. So, Charlie is smart.\n- From statement 15: smart \u2192 big. But Charlie is already big, so that's consistent.\n- Charlie is furry (statement 3).\n- From statement 19: (smart \u2227 furry) \u2192 big. Charlie is smart and furry, so he must be big. Which he is, so that's consistent.\n- Charlie is green (statement 4).\n- From statement 18: green \u2192 smart. So, that's consistent because Charlie is smart.\n- Charlie is round (statement 5).\n- From statement 13: white, big \u2192 round. Charlie is white (statement 6) and big (statement 2), so he must be round, which he is (statement 5). So that's consistent.\n- Charlie is white (statement 6). So, that's a fact.\n\nNow, moving on to Dave:\n\n- Dave is big (statement 7).\n- From statement 12: big \u2192 smart. So, Dave is smart.\n- Dave is quiet (statement 8).\n- From statement 14: quiet \u2192 smart. So, Dave is smart, which matches with statement 12. So, that's consistent.\n\nGary:\n\n- Gary is green (statement 9).\n- From statement 18: green \u2192 smart. So, Gary is smart.\n- Gary is quiet (statement 10).\n- From statement 14: quiet \u2192 smart. So, Gary is smart, which matches with statement 18. So, that's consistent.\n- From statement 16: big, green \u2192 furry. But Gary is green, but is he big? Let's see. From statement 15: smart \u2192 big. Gary is smart (from statement 18), so he must be big. So, Gary is big.\n- So, Gary is big and green, which from statement 16, makes him furry. So, Gary is furry.\n- From statement 11: all furry people are white. So, Gary is furry, so he must be white.\n\nWait, that's a key point. So, Gary is furry, which means he is white (from statement 11). Therefore, Gary is white.\n\nSo, the statement \"Gary is white\" is true.\n\nLet me double-check to make sure I didn't miss anything.\n\nGary is green (statement 9) \u2192 from statement 18, green \u2192 smart. So, Gary is smart. From statement 15, smart \u2192 big. So, Gary is big. From statement 16, big and green \u2192 furry. So, Gary is furry. From statement 11, all furry are white. So, Gary is white.\n\nYes, that seems correct. Therefore, the statement is true.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Gary is white\" is true based on the given context.\n\n**Answer: A) True**",
