 the rabbit, and the rabbit is round. The rabbit visits the dog and the lion. The dog sees the lion, and the lion visits the dog.\n\nNow, let's try to connect these. From fact 20: If something visits the rabbit, then the rabbit needs the bald eagle. So, since the rabbit visits the lion (fact 14) and the rabbit visits the dog (fact 13), according to fact 20, each of these visits to the rabbit implies the rabbit needs the bald eagle. So, the rabbit needs the bald eagle because it visits the lion and the dog.\n\nFrom fact 19: If the rabbit visits the lion and needs the dog, then the dog is round. Since the rabbit does visit the lion (fact 14) and needs the dog (from fact 8 and 9, the lion needs the dog, so maybe the rabbit also needs the dog?), then the dog is round. Wait, fact 19 says if the rabbit visits the lion and needs the dog, then the dog is round. So, since the rabbit visits the lion (fact 14) and the rabbit needs the dog (from fact 20, because the rabbit is visited by the lion, so the rabbit needs the bald eagle, but maybe that's a separate point), then the dog is round. So, the dog is round.\n\nFrom fact 17: If something is rough, then it visits the lion. Since the rabbit is round (fact 12), and from fact 15, if something is round, then it is rough. So, the rabbit is rough. Therefore, the rabbit visits the lion (fact 14). Wait, but fact 14 says the rabbit visits the lion, so that's consistent.\n\nFrom fact 18: If the dog needs the rabbit and the rabbit sees the bald eagle, then the bald eagle sees the dog. So, the dog needs the rabbit (from fact 8, the lion needs the dog, but does that mean the dog needs the rabbit? Or is it the other way around? Wait, fact 8 says the lion needs the dog, so maybe the dog is needed by the lion, but fact 18 says if the dog needs the rabbit, then... So, perhaps the dog needs the rabbit, and the rabbit sees the bald eagle, which would mean the rabbit has seen the bald eagle, so the bald eagle has seen the rabbit? Or does it mean