needs BE.\n\nFrom statement 19: If someone chases the lion, they visit BE. So, C_L \u2192 V_BE.\n\nFrom statement 16: If someone is red, they chase the lion. So, R \u2192 C_L.\n\nFrom statement 5: If someone is red, they chase the lion. So, same as above.\n\nFrom statement 9: If someone chases the tiger, then T chases BE. So, C_T \u2192 T chases BE.\n\nFrom statement 8: If someone chases the lion, they visit BE. So, C_L \u2192 V_BE.\n\nFrom statement 7: If someone visits BE, they need BE.\n\nFrom statement 6: If someone chases the lion and visits BE, they need BE.\n\nSo, let's try to connect these.\n\nWe know that BE visits S (statement 4). So, V_BE is true.\n\nWe also know that the squirrel chases BE (statement 8). So, S chases BE.\n\nWait, statement 8 says the squirrel chases the bald eagle. So, S chases BE.\n\nBut statement 15 says that if someone visits BE and BE visits S, then BE chases S. So, if X visits BE and BE visits S, then BE chases S.\n\nWe know that BE visits S (from statement 4). So, if X visits BE, then BE chases S.\n\nBut who is X? Let's see.\n\nFrom statement 19: If someone chases the lion, they visit BE. So, C_L \u2192 V_BE.\n\nFrom statement 16: If someone is red, they chase the lion. So, R \u2192 C_L.\n\nFrom statement 5: If someone is red, they chase the lion. Same as above.\n\nSo, if someone is red, they chase the lion, which means they visit BE (from 19). So, R \u2192 V_BE.\n\nBut do we know if someone is red? Yes, the tiger is red (statement 11). So, T is red.\n\nFrom statement 16: If someone is red, they chase the lion. So, T is red, so T chases L.\n\nFrom statement 9: If someone chases the tiger, then T chases BE. Wait, no, statement 9 says if someone chases T, then T chases BE. So, C_T \u2192 T chases BE.\n\nBut T is red, and from statement 16, T is red, so T chases L.\n\nWait, but statement 9 says if someone chases T, then T chases BE. So, if someone chases T, then T chases BE. But T is red, so T chases L (from 16). So, does that mean that T chases both L and BE? Or is there a contradiction?\n\nWait, no. Let me clarify. Statement 9: If someone chases T, then T chases BE. So, if X chases T, then T chases BE. So, if X chases T, then T chases BE.\n\nBut from statement 16, T is red, so T chases L. So, T chases both L and BE? Or is it that T chases L because T is red, and if someone else chases T, then T chases BE.\n\nWait, no. Let me parse it again.\n\nStatement 9: If someone chases the tiger (C_T), then the tiger chases the bald eagle (T chases BE). So, C_T \u2192 T chases BE.\n\nStatement 16: If someone is red (R), then they chase the lion (C_L). So, R \u2192 C_L.\n\nStatement 5: Same as 16.\n\nSo, T is red (statement 11). So, T is red, so from 16, T chases L. So, T chases L.\n\nBut from 9, if someone chases T, then T chases BE. But T is already chasing L. So, does that mean T chases both? Or is there a conflict?\n\nWait, perhaps the statements are about different agents. So, someone else chases T, which would cause T to chase BE. But T is already chasing L because T is red.\n\nSo, T chases both L and BE? Or is it that T chases L because T is red, and if someone else chases T, then T chases BE.\n\nI think it's the latter. So, T chases L because T is red, and if someone else chases T, then T chases BE.\n\nBut in the context, we don't have any information that someone else chases T. So, T chases L because T is red, and that's it.\n\nSo, T chases L, and from statement 19, if someone chases L, they visit BE. So, T chases L, so T visits BE.\n\nFrom statement 17: If someone chases L and visits BE, they need BE. So, T chases L and visits BE, so T needs BE.\n\nFrom statement 7: If someone visits BE, they need BE. So, T visits BE, so T needs BE.\n\nSo, T needs BE.\n\nFrom statement 3: The bald eagle needs the tiger. So, BE needs T.\n\nFrom statement 15: If someone visits BE and BE visits S, then BE chases S. We know BE visits S (statement 4). So, if someone visits BE, then BE chases S.\n\nBut who is the someo