s not young.\n\nNow, let's see if we can connect this to Anne.\n\nAnne is furry (point 2). So, from point 10, kind things are furry. Does that mean Anne is kind? Well, point 10 says kind things are furry, but it doesn't necessarily say that furry things are kind. Wait, no, point 10 says kind things are furry, which is a one-way implication. So, if something is kind, it is furry. But it doesn't say that if something is furry, it is kind. So, Anne is furry, but we don't know if she's kind unless we have more info.\n\nWait, but point 8 and 9: nice things are big, and furry things are nice. So, furry things are nice, which are big. So, Anne is furry, so she is nice and big. But wait, does that mean Anne is nice? Let me think.\n\nPoint 10: Kind things are furry. So, kind implies furry. Point 9: Furry implies nice. So, kind implies nice via furry. But does that mean that if something is kind, it's nice, but does being furry imply kind? No, because point 10 only says kind things are furry, not the other way around.\n\nSo, Anne is furry, so she is nice (from point 9). So, Anne is nice. Therefore, the statement \"Anne is nice\" is true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Let's see if there's any contradiction or if I missed something.\n\nAnne is furry, so from point 9, she is nice. So, yes, she is nice. So, the answer should be A) True.\n\nBut wait, let me check if there's any other angle. For example, point 14: If something is kind and not furry, then it's not young. But since Anne is furry, this doesn't apply to her. Point 13: Kind, big things are young. Since Anne is nice (from point 9) and nice things are big (point 8), so Anne is big. So, if Anne is kind and big, she is young (point 13). But do we know if Anne is kind?\n\nWait, point 10 says kind things are furry. Anne is furry, so does that mean she is kind? No, because point 10 is kind implies furry, but not the reverse. So, Anne being furry doesn't necessarily make her kind. So, we can't conclude that Anne is kind, but we can conclude she is nice because she's furry.\n\nWait, but point 9 says furry things are nice. So, regardless of whether they're kind or not, if they're furry, they're nice. So, Anne is nice because she's furry. So, yes, the statement is true.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Anne is nice\" is true because Anne is furry, and according to the context, furry things are nice.\n\n**Answer: A) True**",
