.\n\nPoint 15: If Fiona is not quiet, then Fiona is white. Fiona is quiet (point 6), so this statement doesn't apply to her. But since Fiona is quiet, she's smart (point 7). Also, from point 18: If something is quiet and not smart, then it is not green. Since Fiona is quiet and smart, this doesn't apply to her either.\n\nPoint 16: Smart, white things are big. So if something is smart and white, it's big. But Erin is not white (point 4), so this doesn't apply to Erin.\n\nPoint 17: If something is nice and quiet, then it is big. So if Erin is nice and quiet, she's big. But we don't know if Erin is nice. The context doesn't mention Erin being nice, so this might not apply unless we can infer it.\n\nPoint 18: If something is quiet and not smart, then it is not green. Erin is quiet and smart, so this doesn't apply.\n\nPoint 19: All quiet, smart things are green. Since Erin is quiet and smart, she must be green. Wait, that's a key point. So Erin is green.\n\nPoint 20: If something is big, then it is nice. So if Erin is big, she's nice. But we don't know if she's nice yet.\n\nWait, from point 19, Erin is quiet, smart, and therefore green. So Erin is green. Now, let's see if being green gives us any information about size.\n\nLooking back, point 14 says all green, round things are white. But Erin is green and not white (point 4). So Erin is green and not white. So she's green but not white. Now, point 9 says Gary is green and white. So Gary is green and white. But Erin is green and not white, so she's different from Gary.\n\nNow, let's see if being green relates to being big. Point 9: Gary is big, green, quiet, white. So Gary is big. Point 16: Smart, white things are big. But Erin is not white, so that doesn't apply. Point 17: If something is nice and quiet, then it's big. But we don't know if Erin is nice. However, from point 19, Erin is green, quiet, and smart. So Erin is quiet and smart, which makes her green (point 19). Now, point 17 says if something is nice and quiet,