omething is smart and not young, then it is nice. Bob is smart (statement 2) and, from statement 1, Bob is quiet. But I don't know if Bob is young. Wait, statement 1 says Bob is quiet, but that's it. So Bob's age isn't mentioned. So statement 13 doesn't apply to Bob because we don't know if he's young or not.\n\nWait, but statement 12 says white things are not young. So if something is white, it's not young. But I don't know if Fiona is white. Let me see if I can find any info about Fiona's color.\n\nLooking at the premises, I don't see any direct statement about Fiona's color. However, there are some implications that might relate to her.\n\nFrom statement 8: If something is quiet and white, then it is green. So quiet and white implies green.\n\nFrom statement 10: Green things are white. So if something is green, it's white.\n\nFrom statement 9: All smart things are green. So if something is smart, it's green.\n\nFrom statement 11: All smart, young things are big. So if something is smart and young, it's big.\n\nFrom statement 13: If something is smart and not young, then it's nice.\n\nFrom statement 14: If Bob is green and Bob is nice, then Bob is big.\n\nNow, let's try to see if Fiona can be green or not.\n\nFiona is young (statement 5). Let's see if she can be smart. If Fiona is smart, then from statement 9, she's green. But I don't know if Fiona is smart. The only other info about Fiona is that she's young.\n\nWait, statement 11 says all smart, young things are big. So if Fiona is smart and young, she's big. But I don't know if she's smart. So maybe she's not smart, but I don't know for sure.\n\nAlternatively, let's see if there's any reason Fiona can't be green. From statement 10, green things are white. So if Fiona is green, she's white. But statement 12 says white things are not young. But Fiona is young, so if she were white, that would contradict statement 12. Therefore, Fiona cannot be white because she's young. So if Fiona were green, she would be white, which is impossible because white things aren't young. Therefore, Fiona cannot be green.\n\nWait, that seems like a chain. Let me write it out:\n\nIf Fiona is green (F), then from statement 10, she's white (W). But from statement 12, white things are not young. But Fiona is young (Y). So if F \u2192 W, and W \u2192 \u00acY, but Fiona is Y, so F \u2192 \u00acY, which is a contradiction. Therefore, Fiona cannot be green. So the statement \"Fiona is not green\" must be true.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I didn't make any mistakes in that reasoning.\n\nSo, assuming Fiona is green, she would be white (from 10). But white things are not young (12), but Fiona is young (5). So that's a contradiction. Therefore, Fiona cannot be green. So the statement \"Fiona is not green\" is true.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can approach it differently. Let's see if there's any way Fiona could be green without causing a contradiction.\n\nFrom statement 10: Green \u2192 White.\n\nFrom statement 12: White \u2192 \u00acYoung.\n\nBut Fiona is Young (Y). So if Fiona were green, she would be white, which would mean she's not young, which contradicts her being young. Therefore, Fiona cannot be green. So the statement \"Fiona is not green\" is true.\n\nSo the answer should be A) True.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Fiona is not green\" is true because if Fiona were green, she would have to be white, which contradicts her being young. Therefore, Fiona cannot be green.\n\n**Answer: A) True**",
