ugh.\n\nFact 16: Rough, nice things are quiet. So, if something is nice and rough, it's quiet. But we don't know if Anne is nice or rough.\n\nWait, but let's think about the implications step by step.\n\nFirst, let's see if we can find any connections from Anne's known attributes to \"nice.\"\n\nAnne is green and quiet. From fact 12, quiet things are green, which is consistent. From fact 13, round things are green, so if Anne is green, she could be round, but we don't know.\n\nNow, let's see if there's any way to connect green or quiet to being nice.\n\nFrom fact 14: All nice things are blue. So, to be nice, you have to be blue. But Anne is green, so unless she's also blue, she can't be nice. But we don't know if she's blue.\n\nWait, but let's see if there's any way for Anne to be blue. If she's green, can she be blue? The context doesn't say anything about Anne being blue or not. So, unless there's an implication that green things are blue, which there isn't, we can't assume that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe through some other path. Let's see.\n\nFrom fact 16: Rough, nice things are quiet. So, if something is nice and rough, it's quiet. But we don't know if Anne is nice or rough.\n\nWait, but let's think about Fiona. Fiona is not nice (5), quiet (6), and rough (7). So, Fiona is quiet and rough, but not nice. From fact 16, if something is rough and nice, it's quiet. But Fiona is rough and quiet, but not nice, so that doesn't help us directly.\n\nFrom fact 15: All rough, nice things are blue. So, if something is nice and rough, it's blue. But Fiona is rough and not nice, so she's not blue. So, that doesn't help.\n\nFrom fact 10: If something is round and blue, then it's rough. So, if Anne were round and blue, she'd be rough. But we don't know if she's round or blue.\n\nFrom fact 8: If something is rough and green, then it's round. So, if Anne is green and rough, she's round. But we don't know if she's rough.\n\nFrom fact 9: All smart things are round. So, if Anne is smar