      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I have this logical reasoning problem to solve. Let me try to break it down step by step. The problem gives me a context with several statements and then asks a question about whether a specific statement is true, false, or unknown. The question is: \"The mouse sees the squirrel.\" The options are A) True, B) False, C) Unknown.\n\nFirst, I'll list out all the given statements to understand the relationships between the entities involved. The context is as follows:\n\n1. The lion chases the mouse.\n2. The mouse is cold.\n3. The mouse needs the rabbit.\n4. The rabbit chases the squirrel.\n5. The rabbit sees the lion.\n6. The rabbit sees the squirrel.\n7. The squirrel chases the lion.\n8. If something is cold then it sees the rabbit.\n9. If something sees the rabbit then it sees the squirrel.\n10. If something is cold and it sees the squirrel then the squirrel is cold.\n11. If something chases the squirrel then it needs the squirrel.\n12. If something chases the squirrel and it is not cold then the squirrel does not chase the rabbit.\n13. If the rabbit sees the squirrel and the squirrel chases the rabbit then the rabbit does not chase the squirrel.\n14. If the rabbit chases the squirrel and the squirrel does not chase the rabbit then the squirrel needs the mouse.\n\nWow, that's a lot. Let me try to parse these one by one and see how they connect.\n\nStarting with the facts:\n\n- The lion chases the mouse. So, lion \u2192 mouse.\n- The mouse is cold. So, mouse is cold.\n- The mouse needs the rabbit. So, mouse needs rabbit.\n- The rabbit chases the squirrel. So, rabbit \u2192 squirrel.\n- The rabbit sees the lion. So, rabbit sees lion.\n- The rabbit sees the squirrel. So, rabbit sees squirrel.\n- The squirrel chases the lion. So, squirrel \u2192 lion.\n\nNow, the implications:\n\n8. If something is cold then it sees the rabbit. So, cold \u2192 sees rabbit.\n9. If something sees the rabbit then it sees the squirrel. So, sees rabbit \u2192 