atement: \"If something is furry and blue then it is round.\" Since Fiona is both furry and blue, this would mean she is round.\n\nNow, if Fiona is round, I can check if being round tells us anything about her color. There's a statement: \"If something is round then it is red.\" So, Fiona being round would make her red.\n\nPutting it all together:\n\n- Fiona is green \u2192 Fiona is blue.\n- Fiona is blue \u2192 Fiona is furry.\n- Fiona is furry and blue \u2192 Fiona is round.\n- Fiona is round \u2192 Fiona is red.\n\nTherefore, Fiona is red. So the statement \"Fiona is red\" is true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check to make sure I didn't miss anything. Is there any possibility that Fiona could not be red? Let me see.\n\nAnother approach is to see if there's any contradiction or if any of the premises could lead to a different conclusion. For example, Fiona is green, so she's blue. Blue things are furry, so she's furry. Furthers and blue things are round, so she's round. Round things are red, so she's red. All red, green things are furry, but Fiona is green and red, so that fits. There's no conflicting information here.\n\nSo, yes, Fiona must be red. Therefore, the statement is true.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Fiona is red\" is true based on the given context.\n\n**Answer: A) True**",
