ind, which we already know from statement 3. That doesn't add new info.\n\nFrom statement 17: Young, big things are kind. So, if something is young and big, it's kind. But Bob is kind, so maybe Bob is young and big? Or maybe just one of those.\n\nWait, but we don't know if Bob is young or big. Let me check other statements.\n\nFrom statement 21: If Anne is young and Anne is big, then Anne is nice. But Anne is furry and smart (statements 1 and 2). From statement 14: If Anne is green, then she's big. So, Anne is green \u2192 Anne is big. But we don't know if Anne is green or not. Wait, statement 6 says Charlie is green, not Anne. So, Anne's color isn't specified. So, Anne could be green or not, but we don't know.\n\nWait, but statement 21 is about Anne, not Bob. So, maybe that's not directly relevant.\n\nLooking at Gary: Gary is furry, kind, nice, and young (statements 9-12). So, Gary is a nice, kind, furry, young person. From statement 13: If something is young, then it's nice. So, Gary is young and nice, which fits.\n\nFrom statement 15: Smart things are nice. So, if someone is smart, they're nice. Anne is smart, so she's nice. But Bob isn't mentioned as smart, so maybe Bob isn't nice? Wait, no, statement 4 says Bob is nice. So, Bob is nice, which makes sense because from statement 20, all nice things are kind, so Bob is kind, which we know.\n\nNow, let's think about the attributes related to being big. From statement 5: Charlie is big. Charlie is also green, nice, and young (statements 6,7,8). So, Charlie is a big, green, nice, young person.\n\nFrom statement 17: Young, big things are kind. So, Charlie is young and big, hence kind, which matches statement 5 and 6.\n\nFrom statement 19: If something is nice and big, then it's young. So, if X is nice and big, then X is young. Since Charlie is nice and big, he's young, which fits.\n\nNow, what about Bob? Bob is kind and nice. From statement 17, if someone is young and big, they're kind. But Bob is kind, so maybe Bob is either young and big, or just kind without being big.\n\nWait, but the question is whether Bob is big or not. So, is Bob big? Let's see.\n\nFrom statement 19: Nice and big \u2192 young. So, if Bob is nice and big, he's young. But we don't know if Bob is big. Alternatively, if Bob is not big, then maybe he's not young, but from statement 13, if he's young, he's nice. But Bob is nice regardless, so maybe he's not young.\n\nWait, but statement 13 says if something is young, then it's nice. It doesn't say only nice things are young. So, Bob could be nice without being young.\n\nBut let's see if there's any info that ties Bob to being big. The only other info is that Charlie is big, and Charlie is young, nice, etc. But Bob isn't mentioned as being big or not.\n\nWait, but let's check if Bob could be big. If Bob is big, then from statement 19, since he's nice (statement 4), he would be young. But we don't know if Bob is young. Alternatively, if Bob is not big, then he might not be young, but he's still nice.\n\nBut the question is whether Bob is not big. So, is Bob not big? Or is it possible that Bob is big?\n\nWait, the context doesn't directly state whether Bob is big or not. It only says Bob is kind and nice. So, from the given information, we can't determine if Bob is big or not. Because the only info about being big is about Charlie and Gary isn't mentioned as big.\n\nWait, no, Gary is mentioned as young, but not big. So, the only big person is Charlie. So, Bob could be big or not. But the context doesn't specify.\n\nWait, but let's think again. From statement 19: If something is nice and big, then it's young. So, if Bob is nice and big, he's young. But Bob is nice, so if he's big, he's young. But we don't know if Bob is big.\n\nAlternatively, from statement 17: Young and big \u2192 kind. So, if Bob is young and big, he's kind, which he is. But we don't know if he's young or big.\n\nSo, the problem is that the context doesn't provide any direct information about Bob's size. It only tells us about Charlie being big, but not about Bob. Therefore, we can't determine if Bob is big or not. So, the statement \"Bob is not big\" is unknown because we don't have enough information to confirm or deny it.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. The context says that if something is nice and big, then it's young. So, if Bob is nice and big, he's young. But Bob is nice, so if he's big, he's young. But we don't know if he's big. So, Bob could be big and young, or not big and not young. Since the context doesn't specify, we can't say for sure whether Bob is big or not. Therefore, the statement \"Bob is not big\" is unknown.\n</think>\n\nThe context doesn't provide any direct information about Bob's size, so we can't determine if he's big or not. Therefore, the statement is unknown.\n\nC) Unknown",
