      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I have this logical reasoning problem to solve. Let me try to break it down step by step. The question is whether the statement \"The squirrel does not visit the lion\" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context. The options are A) True, B) False, C) Unknown.\n\nFirst, I'll go through the context provided and list out all the given information. Maybe if I organize it, it'll make more sense.\n\n1. The bald eagle needs the lion.\n2. The bald eagle visits the lion.\n3. The lion is not cold.\n4. The lion does not like the tiger.\n5. The lion visits the tiger.\n6. The squirrel is kind.\n7. The squirrel is young.\n8. The squirrel likes the tiger.\n9. The squirrel does not need the lion.\n10. The squirrel visits the bald eagle.\n11. The tiger is cold.\n12. The tiger visits the bald eagle.\n13. If something visits the bald eagle then it is big.\n14. If something is big then it needs the squirrel.\n15. If something visits the squirrel and it visits the lion then it needs the squirrel.\n16. If something needs the squirrel then it is green.\n17. If something is young then it needs the tiger.\n18. Green things are young.\n19. If something is big and it visits the lion then the lion needs the squirrel.\n20. If the squirrel is big and the squirrel does not like the tiger then the tiger is not big.\n\nAlright, that's a lot. Let me try to parse each of these statements and see what relationships they establish.\n\nStarting with the facts:\n\n- The squirrel is kind (6), young (7), likes the tiger (8), does not need the lion (9), and visits the bald eagle (10). So the squirrel is kind, young, likes the tiger, doesn't need the lion, and goes to the bald eagle.\n\n- The lion is not cold (3), doesn't like the tiger (4), visits the tiger (5), and is visited by the bald eagle (2). So the lion is warm, dislikes the tiger, and the tiger is cold (11). The lion also needs the bald eagle (1), as per the first statement.\n\n- The tiger is cold (11), v