lications, perhaps the squirrel's actions affect others. For example, the squirrel eats the lion (fact 8). So, the squirrel eats the lion, which by fact 14, if someone eats the lion, the lion is red. So, the lion is red.\n\nSince the lion is red, and red people are green (fact 13), the lion is green. Wait, because red people are green, so if the lion is red, then the lion is green.\n\nSo, the lion is green. Now, let's see what happens when someone visits the lion and the lion is green (fact 20). If someone visits the lion and the lion is green, then they are red. So, whoever visits the lion when the lion is green becomes red.\n\nNow, who visits the lion? Let's see:\n\n- The bear visits the lion (fact 4).\n- The squirrel visits the lion (fact 12).\n- The lion visits the bear (fact 7).\n\nSo, the bear and the squirrel both visit the lion. Since the lion is green, both the bear and the squirrel, when they visit the lion, become red.\n\nBut wait, the bear is already stated to be rough (fact 3). If the bear becomes red, then by fact 13, red people are green, so the bear is green. But the bear is already stated to be rough (fact 3). So, the bear is both rough and green? That seems possible unless there's a contradiction.\n\nWait, but the bear is rough (fact 3), and if the bear is red (because they visited the lion, which is green), then the bear is green (fact 13). So, the bear is both rough and green. Is that a problem? Not necessarily, unless there's a rule that says a person can't be both, but the context doesn't specify that.\n\nSimilarly, the squirrel visits the lion, which is green, so the squirrel becomes red (fact 20). Therefore, the squirrel is red, which makes it green (fact 13). So, the squirrel is both rough (fact 10) and green.\n\nWait, but the squirrel is rough (fact 10), and now it's also green. So, the statement \"The squirrel is not rough\" would be false because the squirrel is rough.\n\nBut wait, let me make sure I didn't make a mistake. Let's go throug