lf. So, the only other entity that visits the dog is the cat. So, if the cat visits the dog and the cat eats the lion, then the lion is round. But from point 6, the cat eats the lion, so this condition is met. Therefore, the lion is round.\n\nWait, but let me make sure. The cat eats the lion, so the condition is met: something (cat) visits the dog and eats the lion. Therefore, the lion is round. So, the lion is round.\n\nBut wait, the question is whether the lion is not round. So, if the lion is round, then the statement \"The lion is not round\" is false. But let me make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nLet me recap:\n\nFrom point 7: If something visits the dog and it eats the lion, then the lion is round.\n\nWe know that the cat visits the dog (point 10) and the cat eats the lion (from point 6). So, the cat is the something that meets both conditions. Therefore, the lion is round.\n\nSo, the lion is round. Therefore, the statement \"The lion is not round\" is false.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's any other way the lion could be round or not.\n\nFrom point 21: If something visits the dog and it eats the lion, then the lion is round. We have the cat visiting the dog and eating the lion, so the lion is round.\n\nAdditionally, from point 19: Round things are big. So, the lion is round and big.\n\nBut wait, the lion is blue (point 14). The dog is also blue (point 13). So, both the lion and the dog are blue. The dog is big, so the lion is also big because round things are big, and the lion is round.\n\nSo, the lion is big, blue, and round.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"The lion is not round\" is false because the lion is round.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I didn't miss any other conditions that might affect this.\n\nFrom point 3: If the bald eagle visits the lion, then the lion is nice. The bald eagle does visit the lion (point 3), so the lion is nice. So, the lion is nice, blue, big, and round.\n\nSo, all in all, the lion is round, so the statement is false.\n<