 green and kind, not red. So, he's not rough.\n\nWait, but point 14 says all red, round things are young. So, if Gary were red and round, he'd be young. But Gary is green and round. So, he's not red, so point 14 doesn't apply. But point 13 says kind, red things are young. Since Gary is kind, if he's red, he's young. But he's not red, so that doesn't apply either.\n\nWait, but from point 11, Gary is quiet because he's round. From point 15, since he's round and quiet, he's green. So, Gary is green. Now, point 10 says if something is green and red, it's rough. But Gary is green and kind, not red, so he's not rough.\n\nNow, point 16 says all red, rough things are kind. So, Bob is red and rough, and he's kind. That fits. But Gary is green, so that doesn't apply.\n\nSo, Gary is green, kind, round, and quiet. Now, does being green and kind make him young? Let's see. Point 12 says if something is young, then it's round. So, young leads to round, but not the other way around. So, being round doesn't necessarily mean young unless other conditions are met.\n\nPoint 14 says all red, round things are young. So, if something is red and round, it's young. But Gary is green and round, so that doesn't apply. Point 13 says kind, red things are young. So, if something is red and kind, it's young. But Gary is kind and green, not red.\n\nWait, but point 12 says if something is young, then it's round. So, if Gary were young, he'd be round, which he is. But does that mean he's young? Not necessarily, because being round could be due to other reasons.\n\nWait, but point 14 says all red, round things are young. So, if Gary were red and round, he'd be young. But he's green and round. So, maybe he's not young.\n\nAlternatively, point 13 says kind, red things are young. So, if Gary were red and kind, he'd be young. But he's green, so that doesn't apply.\n\nWait, but from point 15, Gary is green because he's round and quiet. So, he's green. Now, point 10 says if something is green and red, it's