 are green. So if Gary is not red, he's not green. Therefore, Gary is not green.\n\nWait, let me make sure. Statement 11: All red people are green. So if someone is red, they are green. But if someone is not red, they might not be green. So Gary is not red, so he's not green. Therefore, Gary is not green.\n\nFrom statement 8: If Gary is green, then he's not blue. But since Gary is not green, this implication doesn't tell us anything about his color. So Gary could be any color except green, but we need more info.\n\nBut wait, statement 13: If someone is green, they are white. Since Gary is not green, this doesn't apply to him. So Gary's color is unknown from this.\n\nNow, let's look at statement 14: If someone is blue and white, then they are kind. But Gary is not blue (statement 3), so this doesn't apply to him.\n\nWait, but statement 4 says Gary is kind. So regardless of his color, he's kind. So the statement in question is \"Gary is not kind,\" which would be false because he is kind.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Is there any possibility that Gary is not kind? Statement 4 directly says Gary is kind. So unless there's a contradiction, Gary is kind. So the statement \"Gary is not kind\" would be false.\n\nBut wait, let me check if there's any implication that could make Gary not kind. Statement 14: If someone is blue and white, then they are kind. But Gary is not blue, so that doesn't affect him. Statement 4 is a direct statement, so unless that's false, Gary is kind.\n\nWait, but could statement 4 be false? No, because it's given as a fact. So Gary is kind, making the statement \"Gary is not kind\" false.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Gary is not kind\" is false because Gary is explicitly stated to be kind.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
